/*!
    \page style-reference-buttons.html
    \title Styling Buttons and Input Widgets
    \brief The style elements, states, and options of push buttons, checkboxes,
    radio buttons, tool buttons, combo boxes, spin boxes, sliders, scroll bars,
    and progress bars.

    This page is part of the \l{Widget Style Reference}. For an explanation
    of the element trees, screenshots, and tables, see that page. The common
    state flags and members that every widget sets are listed in
    \l{How a Style Draws a Widget#Common state flags and members}{How a Style
    Draws a Widget}.

    \section1 Push buttons

    \l QPushButton draws a single control element, \c CE_PushButton, with a
    \l QStyleOptionButton. \l QCommonStyle splits it into the bevel, the label,
    and the focus frame:

    \list
    \li \c CE_PushButton
        \list
        \li \c CE_PushButtonBevel (\c PM_ButtonDefaultIndicator,
            \c PM_MenuButtonIndicator)
            \list
            \li \c PE_FrameDefaultButton, for the default button
            \li \c PE_PanelButtonCommand
            \li \c PE_IndicatorArrowDown, for a button with a menu
            \endlist
        \li \c CE_PushButtonLabel (\c SE_PushButtonContents,
            \c PM_ButtonShiftHorizontal, \c PM_ButtonShiftVertical,
            \c PM_ButtonIconSize)
        \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect (\c SE_PushButtonFocusRect)
        \endlist
    \endlist

    The button calculates its size hint with \c CT_PushButton and
    \c PM_ButtonMargin. Element bounds vary between styles. In \l QCommonStyle,
    \c PE_FrameDefaultButton gets the whole bevel rectangle, and so does
    \c PE_PanelButtonCommand, except on an auto-default button, where the panel
    shrinks by \c PM_ButtonDefaultIndicator on each side.

    \image styles/pushbutton.webp
           {Two push buttons with the bevel, label, and focus rectangle
           outlined}

    \l QPushButton sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The button is pressed, or its menu is shown.
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The button is checked.
        \row
            \li \c State_Raised
            \li The button isn't flat and isn't pressed.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionButton are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li features
            \li Flags of the \l QStyleOptionButton::ButtonFeature enum that
                describe the button: flat, default, auto-default, with a menu,
                or a command link button.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The button's \l QIcon, if any.
        \row
            \li iconSize
            \li The size of the icon.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The button text.
    \endtable

    \section1 Checkboxes and radio buttons

    \l QCheckBox and \l QRadioButton have identical structures. Both use
    \l QStyleOptionButton and draw one control element, \c CE_CheckBox or
    \c CE_RadioButton. \l QCommonStyle draws the indicator, the label, and the
    focus frame:

    \list
    \li \c CE_CheckBox
        \list
        \li \c PE_IndicatorCheckBox (\c SE_CheckBoxIndicator,
            \c PM_IndicatorWidth, \c PM_IndicatorHeight)
        \li \c CE_CheckBoxLabel (\c SE_CheckBoxContents,
            \c PM_CheckBoxLabelSpacing)
        \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect (\c SE_CheckBoxFocusRect)
        \endlist
    \li \c CE_RadioButton
        \list
        \li \c PE_IndicatorRadioButton (\c SE_RadioButtonIndicator,
            \c PM_ExclusiveIndicatorWidth, \c PM_ExclusiveIndicatorHeight)
        \li \c CE_RadioButtonLabel (\c SE_RadioButtonContents,
            \c PM_RadioButtonLabelSpacing)
        \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect (\c SE_RadioButtonFocusRect)
        \endlist
    \endlist

    The size hints use \c CT_CheckBox and \c CT_RadioButton.
    \l{Styling a Checkbox: A Walkthrough} traces the drawing of a checkbox
    through the widget and \l QCommonStyle code.

    \image styles/checkbox.webp
           {Checkbox and radio button with the indicator, label, and focus
           rectangle outlined}

    The buttons set these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The button is pressed. The clickable area is
                \c SE_CheckBoxClickRect or \c SE_RadioButtonClickRect, which in
                \l QCommonStyle covers the label as well as the indicator.
        \row
            \li \c State_NoChange
            \li The checkbox is partially checked (a tristate checkbox).
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The button is checked.
        \row
            \li \c State_Off
            \li The button is unchecked.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionButton are listed under
    \l{Push buttons}.

    \section1 Tool buttons

    \l QToolButton draws one complex control, \c CC_ToolButton, with a
    \l QStyleOptionToolButton. It has two subcontrols: the button itself and,
    for a button with \l{QToolButton::MenuButtonPopup}{a menu button}, the menu
    indicator.

    \list
    \li \c CC_ToolButton
        \list
        \li \c SC_ToolButton
            \list
            \li \c PE_PanelButtonTool (\c PM_DefaultFrameWidth)
            \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect
            \li \c CE_ToolButtonLabel (\c PM_ButtonShiftHorizontal,
                \c PM_ButtonShiftVertical)
            \endlist
        \li \c SC_ToolButtonMenu (\c PM_MenuButtonIndicator)
            \list
            \li \c PE_IndicatorButtonDropDown
            \li \c PE_IndicatorArrowDown
            \endlist
        \endlist
    \endlist

    For \l{QToolButton::InstantPopup}{instant} and
    \l{QToolButton::DelayedPopup}{delayed} popups there is no menu subcontrol,
    so \l QCommonStyle draws \c PE_IndicatorArrowDown in the corner of the
    button rectangle instead. The size hint uses \c CT_ToolButton, and the
    button consults \c SH_ToolButton_PopupDelay and \c SH_ToolButtonStyle. Tool
    buttons in a toolbar take their icon size from the toolbar. Standalone
    buttons use \c PM_ButtonIconSize.

    \image styles/toolbutton.webp
           {Tool button with an icon, text, and menu indicator, with the button
           and the menu subcontrol outlined}

    \l QToolButton sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_AutoRaise
            \li The \l{QToolButton::autoRaise}{autoRaise} property is set.
        \row
            \li \c State_Raised
            \li The button isn't checked or pressed. \l QCommonStyle then clears
                the flag again if \c State_AutoRaise is set and the mouse isn't
                over the button.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The button is pressed, or its menu is shown.
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The button is checkable and checked.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionToolButton are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li arrowType
            \li A \l{Qt::ArrowType} value that gives the direction of the arrow
                drawn instead of an icon, if any.
        \row
            \li features
            \li Flags of the \l QStyleOptionToolButton::ToolButtonFeature enum:
                whether the button shows an arrow, has a menu button, or has a
                delayed popup.
        \row
            \li font
            \li The \l QFont of the button label.
        \row
            \li icon
            \li The \l QIcon of the button.
        \row
            \li iconSize
            \li The size of the icon.
        \row
            \li pos
            \li The position of the button, as given by \l QWidget::pos().
        \row
            \li text
            \li The button text.
        \row
            \li toolButtonStyle
            \li A \l{Qt::ToolButtonStyle} value that decides whether the button
                shows the icon, the text, or both.
    \endtable

    \section1 Combo boxes

    \l QComboBox draws the button and the label with \c CC_ComboBox and
    \c CE_ComboBoxLabel, both with a \l QStyleOptionComboBox. \l QCommonStyle
    omits the text of the label for an editable box, where the \l QLineEdit
    child draws it. The popup list is an item view drawn by a
    \l{Delegate Classes}{delegate}, but the style controls its size and position
    with \c SC_ComboBoxListBoxPopup. For an editable box, the style decides
    where the line edit goes with \c SC_ComboBoxEditField; the field itself is a
    \l QLineEdit child.

    \list
    \li \c CC_ComboBox (\c PM_ComboBoxFrameWidth)
        \list
        \li \c SC_ComboBoxFrame
        \li \c SC_ComboBoxArrow (\c PE_IndicatorArrowDown)
        \li \c SC_ComboBoxEditField
        \li \c SC_ComboBoxListBoxPopup, for positioning the popup
        \endlist
    \li \c CE_ComboBoxLabel (\c SC_ComboBoxEditField)
    \endlist

    The size hint uses \c CT_ComboBox. Style hints such as \c SH_ComboBox_Popup,
    \c SH_ComboBox_PopupFrameStyle, and \c SH_ComboBox_UseNativePopup decide how
    the popup looks and behaves.

    \image styles/combobox.webp
           {Combo box with the frame, edit field, and arrow subcontrols
           outlined}

    \l QComboBox sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Selected
            \li The box isn't editable and has focus.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li \c SC_ComboBoxArrow is active.
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The popup list is visible.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionComboBox are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li currentIcon
            \li The icon of the current item.
        \row
            \li currentText
            \li The text of the current item.
        \row
            \li editable
            \li Whether the combo box is editable.
        \row
            \li frame
            \li Whether the combo box has a frame.
        \row
            \li iconSize
            \li The size of the current item's icon.
        \row
            \li popupRect
            \li The bounding rectangle of the popup list.
        \row
            \li textAlignment
            \li The alignment of the text in the label.
    \endtable

    \section1 Spin boxes

    \l QSpinBox, \l QDoubleSpinBox, and \l QDateTimeEdit draw \c CC_SpinBox with
    a \l QStyleOptionSpinBox. The edit field is a \l QLineEdit child whose
    geometry the style returns for \c SC_SpinBoxEditField.

    \list
    \li \c CC_SpinBox (\c PM_SpinBoxFrameWidth)
        \list
        \li \c SC_SpinBoxFrame
        \li \c SC_SpinBoxUp
            \list
            \li \c PE_PanelButtonBevel
            \li \c PE_IndicatorSpinUp or \c PE_IndicatorSpinPlus, depending on
                \l{QAbstractSpinBox::buttonSymbols}{buttonSymbols}
            \endlist
        \li \c SC_SpinBoxDown
            \list
            \li \c PE_PanelButtonBevel
            \li \c PE_IndicatorSpinDown or \c PE_IndicatorSpinMinus
            \endlist
        \li \c SC_SpinBoxEditField
        \endlist
    \endlist

    A style doesn't have to draw the button panels with \c PE_PanelButtonBevel.
    The size hint uses \c CT_SpinBox, and the widget consults hints such as
    \c SH_SpinBox_ButtonsInsideFrame, \c SH_SpinBox_StepModifier, and
    \c SH_SpinControls_DisableOnBounds.

    \image styles/spinbox.webp
           {Spin box with the frame, edit field, up button, and down button sub
           controls outlined}

    The spin box sets this state flag:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li The \c SC_SpinBoxUp or \c SC_SpinBoxDown subcontrol is pressed.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionSpinBox are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li frame
            \li Whether the spin box draws a frame.
        \row
            \li buttonSymbols
            \li A \l QAbstractSpinBox::ButtonSymbols value that selects the
                symbols on the buttons: arrows, plus and minus, or none.
        \row
            \li stepEnabled
            \li \l QAbstractSpinBox::StepEnabled flags that tell which of the
                buttons can step the value. A button that can't step is drawn
                disabled.
    \endtable

    \section1 Sliders

    \l QSlider draws \c CC_Slider with a \l QStyleOptionSlider. It calculates
    its size hint from \c PM_SliderThickness and \c CT_Slider, and its minimum
    size hint from \c PM_SliderLength.

    \list
    \li \c CC_Slider (\c PM_SliderLength, \c PM_SliderControlThickness,
        \c PM_SliderTickmarkOffset, \c PM_SliderSpaceAvailable)
        \list
        \li \c SC_SliderGroove
        \li \c SC_SliderHandle
        \li \c SC_SliderTickmarks
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \l QCommonStyle draws only the tick marks; the groove and the handle are
    always style specific. Styles don't have to return a rectangle for
    \c SC_SliderTickmarks. Fusion returns an empty one and draws the ticks below
    the handle, in the area outlined in the screenshot. The widget consults
    \c SH_Slider_AbsoluteSetButtons, \c SH_Slider_PageSetButtons, and
    \c SH_Slider_SnapToValue.

    \image styles/slider.webp
           {Horizontal slider with tick marks, with the groove, handle, and tick
           mark subcontrols outlined}

    \l QDial uses the same option and draws \c CC_Dial with the subcontrols
    \c SC_DialGroove, \c SC_DialHandle, and \c SC_DialTickmarks.

    \l QSlider sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li The slider is horizontal.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li Any subcontrol is pressed, including the groove.
                \c activeSubControls tells which one.
    \endtable

    \l QStyleOptionSlider serves all \l{QAbstractSlider}s. Its members are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li orientation
            \li A \l{Qt::Orientation} value: vertical or horizontal.
        \row
            \li minimum
            \li The minimum value.
        \row
            \li maximum
            \li The maximum value.
        \row
            \li tickPosition
            \li A \l QSlider::TickPosition value that tells where the tick marks
                are drawn.
        \row
            \li tickInterval
            \li The distance between tick marks, in slider values.
        \row
            \li upsideDown
            \li The direction in which the value increases. All abstract sliders
                use this member instead of \l QStyleOption::direction.
        \row
            \li sliderPosition
            \li The position of the handle, as a slider value. It equals
                \c sliderValue while \l{QAbstractSlider::tracking}{tracking} is
                on; otherwise the value only updates when the handle is
                released.
        \row
            \li sliderValue
            \li The current value.
        \row
            \li singleStep
            \li The amount the value changes on a single step, such as an arrow
                key press.
        \row
            \li pageStep
            \li The amount the value changes on a page step, such as a click in
                the groove.
        \row
            \li notchTarget
            \li The preferred distance between notches, in pixels. Used by
                \l QDial.
        \row
            \li dialWrapping
            \li Whether the dial wraps around. Used by \l QDial.
        \row
            \li keyboardModifiers
            \li The modifier keys that were held during the last mouse event,
                for styles that let modifiers change the drag behavior.
    \endtable

    \section1 Scroll bars

    \l QScrollBar draws \c CC_ScrollBar with a \l QStyleOptionSlider. While the
    user drags the slider, the bar snaps the value back if the pointer moves
    farther than \c PM_MaximumDragDistance outside the bar.

    \list
    \li \c CC_ScrollBar (\c PM_ScrollBarExtent, \c PM_ScrollBarSliderMin)
        \list
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarGroove
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarSubLine (\c CE_ScrollBarSubLine)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarAddLine (\c CE_ScrollBarAddLine)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarSubPage (\c CE_ScrollBarSubPage)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarAddPage (\c CE_ScrollBarAddPage)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarFirst (\c CE_ScrollBarFirst)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarLast (\c CE_ScrollBarLast)
        \li \c SC_ScrollBarSlider (\c CE_ScrollBarSlider)
        \li \c PE_FrameFocusRect
        \endlist
    \endlist

    \l QCommonStyle draws each subcontrol with the control element of the same
    name. Some styles draw the line indicators with \c PE_IndicatorArrowUp and
    the other arrow primitives, and the page areas with \c PE_PanelButtonBevel;
    that is up to the individual style. \c SC_ScrollBarFirst and
    \c SC_ScrollBarLast are optional buttons that jump to the ends; most styles
    return an empty rectangle for them. The size hint uses \c CT_ScrollBar, and
    behavior hints include \c SH_ScrollBar_LeftClickAbsolutePosition,
    \c SH_ScrollBar_ContextMenu, and \c SH_ScrollBar_Transient.

    \image styles/scrollbar.webp
           {Horizontal scroll bar with the groove, line buttons, page areas, and
           slider subcontrols outlined}

    \l QScrollBar sets these state flags:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li The scroll bar is horizontal.
        \row
            \li \c State_Sunken
            \li A subcontrol is pressed and the pointer hasn't left it.
        \row
            \li \c State_On
            \li The bar is a transient scroll bar that is currently shown.
    \endtable

    The members of \l QStyleOptionSlider are listed under \l{Sliders}.
    \c sliderPosition, \c sliderValue, \c pageStep, and \c upsideDown decide the
    size and position of the handle.

    \section1 Progress bars

    \l QProgressBar draws one control element, \c CE_ProgressBar, with a
    \l QStyleOptionProgressBar. \l QCommonStyle splits it into the groove, the
    contents, and the label:

    \list
    \li \c CE_ProgressBar
        \list
        \li \c CE_ProgressBarGroove (\c SE_ProgressBarGroove)
        \li \c CE_ProgressBarContents (\c SE_ProgressBarContents,
            \c PM_ProgressBarChunkWidth)
        \li \c CE_ProgressBarLabel (\c SE_ProgressBarLabel)
        \endlist
    \endlist

    Styles that draw the contents as a row of chunks use
    \c PE_IndicatorProgressChunk. In \l QCommonStyle and Fusion, the groove, the
    contents, and the label all get the whole bar; the label is centered text
    drawn over the contents, so its rectangle isn't a separate area. The size
    hint uses \c CT_ProgressBar. A busy indicator, a bar whose minimum and
    maximum are both zero, is animated by the style.

    \image styles/progressbar.webp
           {Progress bar at 40% with the groove, contents, and label outlined}

    \l QProgressBar sets this state flag:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li State
            \li Set when
        \row
            \li \c State_Horizontal
            \li The bar is horizontal.
    \endtable

    The other members of \l QStyleOptionProgressBar are:

    \table 90%
        \header
            \li Member
            \li Description
        \row
            \li minimum
            \li The minimum value.
        \row
            \li maximum
            \li The maximum value.
        \row
            \li progress
            \li The current value.
        \row
            \li text
            \li The label text.
        \row
            \li textAlignment
            \li The alignment of the text in the label.
        \row
            \li textVisible
            \li Whether the label is drawn.
        \row
            \li invertedAppearance
            \li Whether the bar fills from the opposite end, for example, from
                right to left in a horizontal bar.
        \row
            \li bottomToTop
            \li Whether the label of a vertical bar is rotated to read from
                bottom to top.
    \endtable

    \sa {Widget Style Reference}, {Styling Containers and Windows},
        {Styling Menus and Item Views}
*/
